{"name":"Pale Spring Bud","hex":"#ecebbd","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"pale","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Light in color.","Having a pallor (a light color, especially due to sickness, shock, fright etc.)."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To turn pale; to lose colour.","To become insignificant."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/pale"},{"term":"spring","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To move or burst forth.","To appear."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["An act of springing: a leap, a jump.","The season of the year in temperate regions in which temperatures and daylight hours rise, and plants spring from the ground and into bloom and dormant animals spring to life."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/spring"},{"term":"bud","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Symbol","definitions":["ISO 639-3 language code for Ntcham."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A newly sprouted leaf or blossom that has not yet unfolded.","Something that has begun to develop."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/bud"}],"entries":[{"title":"Spring bud","blocks":[{"text":"Spring bud is the color that used to be called spring green before the X11 web color spring green was formulated in 1987 when the X11 colors were first promulgated. This color is now called spring bud to avoid confusion with the web color."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Spring_bud","wikidata":"Q7580765"},{"title":"Bud","blocks":[{"text":"In botany, a bud is an undeveloped or embryonic shoot and normally occurs in the axil of a leaf or at the tip of a stem. Once formed, a bud may remain for some time in a dormant condition, or it may form a shoot immediately. Buds may be specialized to develop flowers or short shoots or may have the potential for general shoot development. The term bud is also used in zoology, where it refers to an outgrowth from the body which can develop into a new individual."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/ba/Fagus_sylvatica_bud.jpg/330px-Fagus_sylvatica_bud.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Bud","wikidata":"Q189838"},{"title":"Colorfulness","blocks":[{"text":"Colorfulness, chroma and saturation are attributes of perceived color relating to chromatic intensity. As defined formally by the International Commission on Illumination (CIE) they respectively describe three different aspects of chromatic intensity, but the terms are often used loosely and interchangeably in contexts where these aspects are not clearly distinguished. The precise meanings of the terms vary by what other functions they are dependent on."},{"items":["Colorfulness is the \"attribute of a visual perception according to which the perceived color of an area appears to be more or less chromatic \". The colorfulness evoked by an object depends not only on its spectral reflectance but also on the strength of the illumination, and increases with the latter unless the brightness is very high.","Chroma is the \"colorfulness of an area judged as a proportion of the brightness of a similarly illuminated area that appears white or highly transmitting\". As a result, chroma is mostly only dependent on the spectral properties, and as such is seen to describe the object color. It is how different from a grey of the same lightness such an object color appears to be.","Saturation is the \"colorfulness of an area judged in proportion to its brightness\", which in effect is the perceived freedom from whitishness of the light coming from the area. An object with a given spectral reflectance exhibits approximately constant saturation for all levels of illumination, unless the brightness is very high."]}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5d/Brightness_and_colorfulness.png/330px-Brightness_and_colorfulness.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colorfulness","wikidata":"Q27673"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Halesia_carolina%2C_skubblare%2C_Manie_van_der_Schijff_BT%2C_a.jpg/500px-Halesia_carolina%2C_skubblare%2C_Manie_van_der_Schijff_BT%2C_a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Halesia_carolina%2C_skubblare%2C_Manie_van_der_Schijff_BT%2C_a.jpg/1280px-Halesia_carolina%2C_skubblare%2C_Manie_van_der_Schijff_BT%2C_a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Halesia_carolina%2C_skubblare%2C_Manie_van_der_Schijff_BT%2C_a.jpg/500px-Halesia_carolina%2C_skubblare%2C_Manie_van_der_Schijff_BT%2C_a.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Inflorescence bud scales in Halesia carolina","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHalesia_carolina%2C_skubblare%2C_Manie_van_der_Schijff_BT%2C_a.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Ficus_bud.JPG/500px-Ficus_bud.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Ficus_bud.JPG/1280px-Ficus_bud.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8d/Ficus_bud.JPG/500px-Ficus_bud.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Terminal, vegetative bud of Ficus carica","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AFicus_bud.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Alnus_glutinosa_bud_2.jpg/250px-Alnus_glutinosa_bud_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Alnus_glutinosa_bud_2.jpg/500px-Alnus_glutino